## 3️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to debug them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Quick check |
|---------|--------------|-------------|
| **No circles appear, but network shows 200s** | `source-layer` name wrong (vector source) or `population` field missing / not numeric. | Open the TileJSON (or GeoJSON) and verify the field name. In the console: `map.querySourceFeatures('mytiles', {sourceLayer: 'places'}).map(f=>f.properties.population)` |
| **Circles appear but all the same colour** | The expression is not being evaluated – maybe you used `["get","population"]` on a raster source, or the property is a string that never converts to a number. | Add `console.log(map.getStyle())` and inspect the `paint` object. Try a static colour first (`'circle-color': '#f00'`) to confirm the layer works. |
| **Colours look wrong (e.g. dark for low values)** | Interpolation direction reversed or stops out of order. | Ensure the stops are **ascending** in the numeric value. |
| **Map is blank, but network shows 404s for tiles** | Wrong tile URL template (`{z}/{x}/{y}` vs `{z}/{x}/{-y-1}`) or CORS. | Open a single tile URL in the browser (replace `{z}/{x}/{y}` with real numbers). |
| **Labels disappear after adding the circle layer** | You added the layer *after* the label layers, so it sits on top. | Use `map.addLayer(layerObj, firstSymbolId)` where `firstSymbolId` is the id of the first `symbol` layer (usually a label). Example: `const firstLabel = map.getStyle().layers.find(l=>l.type==='symbol').id; map.addLayer(circleLayer, firstLabel);` |
| **`setFeatureState` does nothing** | Feature has no `id`. | Add `promoteId: 'population'` (or the appropriate property) to the source definition, or ensure the GeoJSON features have a top-level `id`. |

